# The `hygiene` step for .github/workflows/ci.yml's `check` job. It is not a
# workflow of its own -- it is the two lines below, which go into that job
# immediately after its last step:
#
#       - run: go vet -tags firebase ./...
#
# so that the job ends like this:
#
#       - run: go vet -tags firebase ./...
#       - name: hygiene
#         run: scripts/ci-hygiene.sh
#
# Why they are staged here rather than written into the file: a Fugaro run
# cannot push a `.github/workflows/**` change (GitHub refuses the push), so a
# workflow change is staged in this directory for a person to copy in.
# docs/pending-workflows/README.md's `## ci-hygiene.yml.snippet` section is
# where the copy-in is written out.
#
# What the step runs: scripts/ci-hygiene.sh, one bash script with no
# dependencies beyond git and awk. It fails the job (exit 1, one line per
# violation) on a tracked file whose name ends in -E, .orig, .bak, .rej or ~
# -- the `sed -i` leftovers a pull request committed in the week of 2026-11-10,
# which carry no licence notice and reddened `TestEEFilesCarryTheNotice` and
# `TestDocsEELicensed` on main -- and it prints `warn:` lines, without failing,
# for two shapes that took main's CI red the same week: a test that builds a
# literal-year `time.Date` in a function that also reaches the process clock
# (`TestRotateReturnsAFreshPair` aged out of its 7-day idle bound on
# 2026-11-13) and an assertion that bounds `time.Since(...)` by a literal
# duration under 60s (`TestRedactPassesAreBounded`, under -race).
#
# The `check` job is where it belongs rather than a job of its own: it needs no
# Go toolchain to speak of and no artifact, it finishes in about a second, and
# `check` is already the job a pull request waits on for gofmt and the vet
# passes.

      - name: hygiene
        run: scripts/ci-hygiene.sh
